  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1159 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nSurface observations as of 10PM Saturday depict areas of patchy\nfog developing over the Mid-South. Given clearing skies, light\nwinds, and low dewpoint depressions, dense fog potential will\ncontinue to increase over the next several hours. A Dense Fog\nAdvisory may be warranted if trends hold. Visibilities will\nquickly improve after sunrise.\n\nDangerous heat and humidity will settle over the Mid-South on\nSunday as an upper level ridge, centered over the Rockies,\nstrengthens to 599 dam. Afternoon highs will warm into the low to\nand mid 90s with heat indices reaching as high as 113 degrees.\nUnfortunately, temperatures will continue to warm into next week\nwith a few areas reaching 100 degrees on Tuesday. A Heat Advisory\nand Extreme Heat Warning are in effect through 8PM Tuesday. The\nwarning area may be expanded as we head into Monday and Tuesday.\nAside from the heat and humidity, rain chances cannot be ruled\nout Sunday into Monday as an MCS develops over the Midwest. This\nsystem will propagate southeast, potentially impacting areas\nalong the Tennessee River. Confidence in this occurring is low\ndue to run-to-run model inconsistencies and the placement of the\naforementioned ridge. However, a stray storm or two cannot be\nruled out into Monday.\n\nOn Wednesday, a back door cold front will moderate temperatures\nand dewpoints to the low 90s and upper 60s, respectively. While\nnot providing much relief, this will be enough to decrease the\nneed for heat headlines as we move into the latter half of the\nweek. Long term guidance is hinting at increased rain chances for\nthe upcoming weekend, but it remains too early to tell specifics.\n\nANS",
